      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "NeoTech",
        "email": "andreas@roughedge.se",
        "date": "2026-11-11T23:06:56Z"
      },
      "message": "feat(kernel): STEP import through solidcore-step behind the seam, benched against truck\n\nM12.1 stage 4, bench only: crates/app still imports STEP through\ntruck-stepio; no other workspace crate gains a dependency.\n\n- Pin: crates/kernel takes solidcore, solidcore-step and solidcore-convert\n  from the owner's fork at rev 55d0e7638b9e1ebbf91ab8c10657e595c114ecda\n  (tag camcraft-pin-2026-11-11) in place of crates.io solidcore =1.0.1;\n  solidcore-tess at the same rev is a dev-dependency for the mesh bench.\n  Cargo.lock: five git packages replace the one registry package.\n- Against 1.0.1 (2973 commits): Brep is no longer Clone (two test helpers\n  copy or measure in place); the port now refuses a NaN offset and the\n  native reader refuses blank, foreign, noise and header-only files\n  itself - five pinned port verdicts re-triaged, all former findings.\n- solidcore_step: read_step / import_step - bytes to solids in mm, the\n  file's length units and diagnostics; 64 MiB size bound; the caller's\n  CancelToken as the time bound (KernelError::TimedOut); panics caught;\n  the stage-3 laws (integrity, closed solid, vertex gap) unchanged; an\n  unconvertible length unit and a partly read file are refused by name.\n- Bench: every STEP fixture through both importers, verdicts pinned.\n  Four files agree, three hold no solid and both refuse, and the two\n  Milling-test files disagree on the bounding box (the port's tight box\n  takes the whole cylinder of a trimmed face - #939). A 19-file corrupt\n  corpus through both, under a bound that cancels the import.\n- Mesh: solidcore-tess at the app's 0.005 mm chord is watertight and\n  closer to the exact volume than truck's mesh on every fixture; what a\n  machining mesh still owes is #940.\n\nDecisions in docs/decisions/916-solidcore-step-bench.md; notices in\nassets/ui/LICENSES.md.\n\nFixes #916\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
